Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit of `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs` (MSTest 4.1, .NET 10). Integration-test calibration rules have been applied — the class is marked `[TestCategory("Integration")]`, so Mystery Guest and Eager Test thresholds are adjusted accordingly.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 3           | 3              |
| Medium   | 2           | 2              |
| Low      | 0           | 0              |
| **Total**| **5**       | **4***         |

*\*One test (`NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay`) carries two smells.*

---

## Findings by Severity

### 🔴 HIGH — Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)

**Smell:** A test method exercises production code but makes zero assertions. The framework reports it as "passed" even if the code silently corrupts data.

**Affected test:** `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }
    // ← no assertion — passes even if every Insert silently fails
}
```

**Fix — assert the observable outcome:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
    {
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
    }

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(100, users.Count, "All 100 bulk-inserted users should be persisted.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — 100 % pass rate with 0 % verification. A broken `Insert` path would never be caught.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Conditional Test Logic

**Smell:** `if`/`else` branching inside a test means one path is never exercised for a given run, hiding a testing gap.

**Affected test:** `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

```csharp
var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)        // ← branch A
{
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
}
else                                      // ← branch B
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
}
```

Only **one** branch runs per execution. If the production code changes which type is returned, the other branch's assertion silently disappears.

**Fix — write two explicit tests, each with a deterministic setup:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenStandard_ReturnsUserWithName()
{
    _repository.Insert(new User("eve@example.com", "Eve"));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_WhenPremium_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    _repository.Insert(new PremiumUser("eve@example.com", "Eve", discountRate: 0.1m));
    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0, "Premium users must have a positive discount.");
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** A type-mapping regression could go undetected because the `else` branch happens to still pass.

---

### 🔴 HIGH — Sleepy Test

**Smell:** `Thread.Sleep` introduces a hard-coded wall-clock wait. The test is slow (3 s per run), and the delay may be too short on a loaded CI agent or too long for local development.

**Affected test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← hard 3-second stall

Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```

**Fix — poll with a timeout instead of sleeping:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    // Poll up to 5 s, checking every 100 ms
    var deadline = DateTime.UtcNow.AddSeconds(5);
    while (!_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
    {
        Assert.IsTrue(DateTime.UtcNow < deadline,
            "Notification was not sent within the expected window.");
        await Task.Delay(100);
    }
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky CI failures on slow machines; unnecessarily slow suite on fast ones. This is the #1 academic predictor of flaky tests (Camara et al., SAST 2021).

> **Integration-test calibration note:** Even for integration tests, `Thread.Sleep` remains a **High** smell — the fix (poll/retry) is straightforward and eliminates non-determinism. Only true external-service wait scenarios where polling isn't possible would warrant a downgrade.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Assertion Roulette

**Smell:** A test contains ≥ 3 assertions with no explanatory message parameter. When one fails, the MSTest output says only *"Assert.AreEqual failed. Expected:<Alice>. Actual:<null>"* — the developer must open the source to determine which property was wrong.

**Affected test:** `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase` (line 28) — **4 bare assertions**

```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);                          // which null?
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name);              // name? email?
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email);  // ↑ same issue
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0);                       // what about Id?
```

**Fix — add message parameters:**
```csharp
Assert.IsNotNull(loaded, "User should be retrievable after insert.");
Assert.AreEqual("Alice", loaded.Name, "Persisted name should match.");
Assert.AreEqual("alice@example.com", loaded.Email, "Persisted email should match.");
Assert.IsTrue(loaded.Id > 0, "Database should assign a positive Id.");
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Slower triage in CI — developers waste time re-running or reading source to identify the failing property.

---

### 🟡 MEDIUM — Magic Number Test

**Smell:** An unexplained numeric literal appears in a non-obvious context.

**Affected test:** `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(3000);  // ← Why 3000? SLA? Arbitrary guess?
```

**Fix — extract to a named constant:**
```csharp
private static readonly TimeSpan NotificationTimeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3);
// …
await Task.Delay(NotificationTimeout);
```

> **Calibration note:** `Assert.AreEqual(3, users.Count)` in `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` was **not** flagged — the 3 is self-documenting since exactly 3 items were inserted on the lines immediately above. Likewise `10` in the concurrency test and `0` in `loaded.Id > 0` are contextually clear.

**Risk if unfixed:** Future maintainers can't tell if 3000 ms is a contractual SLA, a race-condition workaround, or an arbitrary pick.

---

## Smells Scanned — No Findings (Clean)

The remaining 14 smells from the testsmells.org catalog were checked and are **clean**:

| Smell | Status | Notes |
|-------|--------|-------|
| Mystery Guest | ✅ Clean | Uses in-memory SQLite — hermetic; integration-calibrated |
| Eager Test | ✅ Clean | No test calls 4+ distinct production methods; integration-calibrated |
| Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean | No `ToString()`-based comparisons |
| Exception Handling | ✅ Clean | No `try`/`catch` blocks in tests |
| General Fixture | ✅ Clean | `_connection` and `_repository` used by every test |
| Ignored/Disabled Test | ✅ Clean | No `[Ignore]` attributes |
| Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean | No repeated identical assertions |
| Lazy Test | ✅ Clean | Shared method calls (`Insert`, `GetByEmail`) serve different test goals |
| Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean | Properly uses `[TestInitialize]` |
| Default Test | ✅ Clean | Class is meaningfully named |
| Redundant Print | ✅ Clean | No `Console.WriteLine` or debug output |
| Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean | No tautological assertions |
| Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean | In-memory DB, no assumed external files |
| Empty Test | ✅ Clean | All methods have executable statements |

## Well-Written Tests Worth Noting

